[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Exception Stubbing and thenThrow() in Mockito"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"When writing unit tests, ensuring that your code gracefully handles error conditions is just as important as testing the happy path. Mockito provides the thenThrow() method to simulate exception throwing when specific methods on mocked objects are invoked."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding thenThrow()"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The thenThrow() method is used in combination with Mockito's when() clause. It allows you to define a specific exception (either checked or unchecked) that should be thrown when a mocked method is called with particular arguments."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"when(mockedList.get(0)).thenThrow(new IndexOutOfBoundsException("List is empty"));"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Stubbing Unchecked (Runtime) Exceptions"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Unchecked exceptions inherit from RuntimeException and do not need to be declared in the method signature. Testing how your application reacts to these failures is straightforward."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"UserRepository mockRepo = mock(UserRepository.class);
when(mockRepo.findById(1L)).thenThrow(new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid user ID"));

// Calling mockRepo.findById(1L) will now throw IllegalArgumentException"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Stubbing Checked Exceptions"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Checked exceptions must be handled or declared. When stubbing a checked exception, ensure that the method you are mocking actually throws that exception in its signature, otherwise Mockito will throw a WrongTypeOfReturnValue exception."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"ExternalService mockService = mock(ExternalService.class);
when(mockService.connect()).thenThrow(new java.io.IOException("Connection timeout"));"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Stubbing Void Methods"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Because you cannot use when() with void return types, Mockito provides the doThrow() method for exception stubbing on void methods."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"EmailService mockEmail = mock(EmailService.class);
doThrow(new RuntimeException("Mail server down")).when(mockEmail).sendEmail(anyString());"},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use thenThrow() for methods that return values by pairing it with when()."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use doThrow() for void methods or when dealing with spy objects."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always match the exception type to what the calling code expects or what the method signature permits."}]
